<p>A database connection carries authority. If it can read every shop’s orders, any SQL running through it can ask for every shop’s orders. If it owns the tables, it may also be able to change their definitions and remove their protections. A correctly bound parameter does not reduce either power.</p>
<p><em>Least privilege</em> means giving an identity the authority its task requires, and making the boundary small enough to test. A catalogue worker needs to read and edit products. A migration process needs to change table definitions. A support tool may need a limited report. Sharing an owner login between all three makes the most exposed task carry the most powerful credentials.</p>
<p>The examples use PostgreSQL 18 and the collection’s tenant-scoped shop variant. Shop 10 is the original shop; shop 20 is Harbour Tea. Each has a P7, but they are different products. We will derive separate runtime, migration and support access, then test what a shop 10 runtime identity can do when it deliberately asks for shop 20.</p>

<h2 id="start-from-the-task">Defining access requirements</h2>
<p>Begin with operations and data, before role names. For this example the runtime task is catalogue editing: list products, add a product and change its catalogue price inside one authorised shop. It does not delete products, change their identity or alter the schema. Checkout stock changes and payment processing would be separate tasks with their own grants.</p>
<p>The migration process creates and changes the objects used by those tasks. The support tool lists product IDs, names and prices across both shops, deliberately. It has no editing task. This is a product-access example; no customer or payment data is needed by any of its reports.</p>
<table><caption>Authority derived from these three tasks</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Task</th><th scope="col">Needs</th><th scope="col">Boundary to test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Catalogue runtime</td><td>Read products, insert permitted fields, update price</td><td>One shop; no deletes, key changes or schema changes</td></tr><tr><td>Migration</td><td>Act as the owner while applying a deployment</td><td>Powerful path unavailable to runtime; not used for ordinary requests</td></tr><tr><td>Support catalogue</td><td>Read an explicitly all-shop product projection</td><td>View only; no underlying table grant or writes</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>A SQL privilege is usually coarser than a business operation. Permission to update price allows arbitrary valid price updates within its row scope; it does not prove that a human approved the price. Likewise, permission to insert an order is not a complete checkout protocol. Keep application authorisation, constraints and transaction rules alongside the database boundary.</p>
<p>There are several scopes to choose: which database, which schema, which object, which command, which columns and which rows. “Read-only” answers only the command question. A read-only login that sees every tenant’s private records may still have far too much authority.</p>

<h2 id="separate-ownership-from-runtime">Ownership and runtime roles</h2>
<p>In PostgreSQL, a <em>role</em> can own objects, hold privileges and be a member of other roles. A role with <code>LOGIN</code> can be a connection identity. A role without it is useful for grouping privileges or holding ownership, but members may still be able to act through it.</p>
<p>The experiment gives table ownership to <code>shop_owner</code>, a role without a login. A separate <code>migration</code> login can deliberately assume that owner role. Runtime logins cannot. This preserves a clear distinction between ordinary catalogue work and the power to redefine the catalogue’s rules.</p>
<Sql label="Separate the owner from the deployed logins" code={`CREATE ROLE shop_owner NOLOGIN;
CREATE ROLE migration LOGIN NOINHERIT;
CREATE ROLE catalogue NOLOGIN;
CREATE ROLE runtime_10 LOGIN;
CREATE ROLE runtime_20 LOGIN;

GRANT shop_owner TO migration
  WITH INHERIT FALSE, SET TRUE;
GRANT catalogue TO runtime_10, runtime_20
  WITH INHERIT TRUE, SET FALSE;`} />
<p>The migration login does not automatically inherit ownership in this setup. It can use <code>SET ROLE shop_owner</code> for the deployment. The runtime logins inherit the catalogue grants while keeping their own effective identity. <code>SET FALSE</code> prevents them assuming the shared group role. They have no membership path to the owner or to each other, and no superuser, role-creation or row-security bypass attribute.</p>
<p>The distinction is authority, not just a nicer name. An owner can change a table’s definition and normally bypasses its row security. Revoking an owner’s ordinary <code>SELECT</code> grant does not turn ownership into a limited runtime identity: the owner can restore its privileges. A role that can become the owner carries that power too.</p>
<p>Use separate deployment credentials and access paths so ordinary application configuration cannot silently select the migration login. A runtime process that can retrieve the owner’s credential is not contained by the grants on its nominal login. The same reasoning applies to a shared service account used by both a support console and a deployment job.</p>

<h2 id="grant-the-objects-the-task-uses">Object and column privileges</h2>
<p>The tenant product table is a projection of the shared example: <code>shop_id</code>, <code>product_id</code>, <code>name</code> and <code>price</code>, with primary key <code>(shop_id, product_id)</code>. Shop 10 has the three core products. Shop 20 has its Copper mug, P7 at 18.00. Stock is omitted because this task does not change it.</p>
<p>Objects live in schema <code>shop</code>. To use the products table, the catalogue role needs schema lookup permission as well as table privileges:</p>
<Sql label="Grant the catalogue task its object and column permissions" code={`GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA shop TO catalogue;
GRANT SELECT ON shop.products TO catalogue;
GRANT INSERT (shop_id, product_id, name, price),
      UPDATE (price)
ON shop.products TO catalogue;`} />
<p><code>USAGE</code> permits looking up objects in the schema. It is not permission to read every table there. <code>UPDATE (price)</code> permits assigning price, while <code>SELECT</code> permits reading the columns used to find a product and calculate its replacement. No <code>DELETE</code>, <code>TRUNCATE</code> or schema <code>CREATE</code> is granted.</p>
<p>Generated identifiers can introduce another object: a sequence whose <code>nextval</code> call needs permission. Functions can introduce an <code>EXECUTE</code> privilege. Derive those dependencies from the commands the task really runs; “it can update the table” does not establish that every helper it invokes is available.</p>
<p>Also inspect privileges granted to <code>PUBLIC</code>, PostgreSQL’s all-roles group. Effective authority combines direct grants, inherited roles, public grants and special attributes. Revoking a direct grant cannot remove the same permission still inherited through another path. The experiment revokes public database access, grants the named logins connection permission and denies public creation in the shared schema.</p>
<p>A schema name is a namespace, not a sealed tenant boundary. A caller with access can qualify another schema’s table directly. Keep untrusted object creation out of schemas that trusted code searches: allowing someone to create a function or table where another task resolves unqualified names can change which object that task uses.</p>

<h2 id="a-shop-label-is-not-authority">Tenant identity and row security</h2>
<p>The table key makes <code>(10, P7)</code> and <code>(20, P7)</code> distinct. Scoped foreign keys keep stored relationships inside one shop. Neither rule stops a well-formed query from requesting the other shop. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/constraints/">Constraints and tenant boundaries</a> separates that integrity question from authorisation.</p>
<p>With the grants above and no row policy, either runtime login can read all four product rows. Adding <code>WHERE shop_id = 10</code> to the application’s usual query narrows that query. It does not prevent a missing condition, a support query or arbitrary SQL under the same connection from reading shop 20.</p>
<p>PostgreSQL row security can impose a row condition at the table. But the condition is only as trustworthy as the identity it uses. Consider a policy that compares the row’s shop with <code>current_setting('app.shop_id')</code>. If this runtime login can set that value, it can choose 20. A policy now exists, but the caller still chooses whose data it sees.</p>
<p>In Figure 1, keep the database login at <code>runtime_10</code> and change the caller setting to 20. Then switch the policy to Database identity and try the same change.</p>
<TenantAuthority />
<p>The first rule returns the Copper mug to the shop 10 runtime. The second keeps returning the Blue mug. A database identity that the runtime cannot replace supplies the scope; a text value supplied by that same runtime does not.</p>
<p>A trusted application backend may intentionally use one database login for many shops. It authenticates the web caller, establishes the authorised shop and supplies a transaction-local context for its SQL. That can protect against accidentally omitted filters, provided the context is set and cleared correctly. It does not isolate tenants from arbitrary SQL running with a credential that can choose any shop context. State which of those threats the design covers.</p>

<h2 id="bind-the-policy-to-the-real-caller">Row policies and database identity</h2>
<p>The native example instead uses two runtime logins, one per shop. The trusted backend selects the appropriate connection credentials; an incoming request cannot select another login. Each login inherits the same command privileges but cannot become the other. This is a small, explicit identity model, not a recommendation to create an unbounded number of tenant connection pools.</p>
<Sql label="A policy scoped by the effective database identity" code={`ALTER TABLE shop.products ENABLE ROW LEVEL SECURITY;

CREATE POLICY tenant_products
ON shop.products TO catalogue
USING (
  shop_id = CASE current_user
    WHEN 'runtime_10' THEN 10
    WHEN 'runtime_20' THEN 20
  END
)
WITH CHECK (
  shop_id = CASE current_user
    WHEN 'runtime_10' THEN 10
    WHEN 'runtime_20' THEN 20
  END
);`} />
<p>The small mapping is fixed by the owner. An unmatched identity receives no true shop comparison. <code>USING</code> restricts existing rows available to commands; <code>WITH CHECK</code> restricts proposed rows. Neither grants the command itself. Both table privileges and an applicable policy are needed.</p>
<p>Through an actual <code>runtime_10</code> login session, the experiment returns three products and the Blue mug at 20.00. <code>runtime_20</code> returns one product and the Copper mug at 18.00. Asking explicitly for shop 20 through runtime_10 returns no rows; an update of those hidden rows affects zero. An attempted shop 20 insert is refused even though its values are well formed. Changing the caller-settable shop setting does not change these outcomes.</p>
<p>A hidden row and an error are different observations. A SELECT may succeed with an empty result, and an UPDATE may succeed with zero affected rows. The application must interpret that result, rather than treating “no SQL exception” as proof that a requested edit happened.</p>
<p>Connection identity also has more than one name. <code>session_user</code> identifies the session identity; <code>current_user</code> identifies the effective execution context. <code>SET ROLE</code> and privileged code can change the latter. The test records both at entry and attempts forbidden role switches. It does not infer safety from a role’s name containing “runtime”.</p>
<p>A proxy or pool can alter that context. If every backend connection runs as an owner while the application merely labels it runtime_10, this policy is not being tested under the intended identity. Verify the effective identity on the actual application connection and test whatever role/context setup the pool performs on checkout. Session settings must not leak to the next borrower; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/application-sql/">SQL at the application boundary</a> follows the ownership of that connection and transaction.</p>

<h2 id="make-privileged-paths-explicit">Privileged access paths</h2>
<p>The migration path is powerful on purpose. When it assumes <code>shop_owner</code>, the experiment can read both shops and alter the table. A superuser can do so too. Their successful reads are controls demonstrating why owner sessions are unsuitable for tenant acceptance tests.</p>
<p>Support has a different deliberate exception. The owner creates a view exposing the named catalogue fields across both shops, then grants support only that view:</p>
<Sql label="Expose the intended all-shop support report" code={`-- Run as shop_owner.
CREATE VIEW support_read.catalogue AS
SELECT shop_id, product_id, name, price
FROM shop.products;

GRANT USAGE ON SCHEMA support_read TO support;
GRANT SELECT ON support_read.catalogue TO support;`} />
<p>In this ordinary PostgreSQL view, underlying access uses the owner’s authority. The owner normally bypasses the table’s row policy, so support intentionally sees all four rows. The runtime logins receive no grant on this view. This is not a way to carry their tenant policy through automatically.</p>
<p>The support login cannot read the base table or update the view in the native test. Listing columns also prevents a future table column from appearing in the view merely because it was added. A <code>security_invoker</code> view has a different privilege model; choose and test the model that implements the intended interface.</p>
<p>Privileged functions, exports, replicas and backups are further paths to the same data. Row policies on ordinary table queries do not establish their authority. Catalogue them separately, including who can call privileged code and who can read copied data. <a href="/learn/databases/relational/postgresql/access-control/">PostgreSQL roles, policies and privileged code</a> develops those PostgreSQL-specific boundaries; <a href="/learn/databases/relational/cockroachdb/operations/">Operating and securing a CockroachDB cluster</a> follows secure identity and native tenant acceptance there.</p>

<h2 id="test-allowed-and-forbidden-work">Testing access boundaries</h2>
<p>Use the real runtime credential and connection setup for acceptance. An administrator issuing a query with <code>WHERE shop_id = 10</code> tests a filter, not runtime containment. Record effective identity, inherited memberships, object ownership, policies and the data that makes each attempted escape distinguishable.</p>
<table><caption>Acceptance cases in the disposable PostgreSQL run</caption><thead><tr><th scope="col">Attempt</th><th scope="col">Expected observation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>runtime_10 reads P7</td><td>Blue mug, 20.00</td></tr><tr><td>runtime_10 asks for shop 20</td><td>No returned row; cross-shop update affects zero</td></tr><tr><td>runtime_10 inserts a shop 20 product</td><td>Refused by row security</td></tr><tr><td>runtime_10 changes a key, truncates or alters the table</td><td>Refused by privileges or ownership</td></tr><tr><td>runtime_10 assumes runtime_20 or owner</td><td>Refused</td></tr><tr><td>support reads the report, then tries the base table</td><td>Report succeeds; base-table access refused</td></tr></tbody></table>
<p>The other-shop row must actually exist. An empty shop 20 would let a broken policy appear correct. Keep a permitted operation too: the run reinserts the core tea product inside shop 10 and performs a price edit that is rolled back. A test suite where every operation is forbidden has not established that the application can do its job.</p>
<p>Counterexamples make the protection visible. Before row security, runtime_10 reads both shops. Under the caller-setting policy, it can select shop 20 itself. After installing the identity policy, that same attempt stays within shop 10. The final fixture retains all original prices.</p>

<h2 id="keep-the-boundary-as-the-system-changes">Grants, revocation and schema changes</h2>
<p>Access must evolve with schema changes. A grant on existing tables does not necessarily cover tomorrow’s table. PostgreSQL default privileges apply to objects created later by the specified creating role; they do not repair existing grants. An object created under the wrong deployment role can therefore get different defaults.</p>
<p>The experiment chooses an explicit object allowlist: a table created later by the migration owner is unreadable to runtime until deliberately granted. If you instead grant runtime access to every future table, also arrange its row policies and sensitive columns before exposure. Broad defaults can turn an additive schema change into an unintended access change.</p>
<p>Revocation needs the same complete view. The run removes the catalogue group’s SELECT grant, verifies that a fresh runtime login loses reading, then restores the scoped result. Revoking a redundant direct grant would prove less. In an incident, separately stop credential reuse, inspect or terminate existing sessions as required, and account for data already exported; removing a grant cannot retract a copied result.</p>
<p>Authentication and transport are another layer. Limit which service can reach the database, authenticate the named identity, verify the server and protect credentials. A shared password copied into a browser is not a tenant identity model. For credential rotation, test new connection establishment and retire the old path, including pools that keep sessions alive. These operational checks are separate from proving SQL privileges.</p>

<h2 id="repeat-the-access-checks">Repeat the access checks</h2>
<p>Download <a href="/review/relational-collection/evidence/r25/run-access.py/" download="run-access.py">the access experiment</a> and <a href="/review/relational-collection/evidence/r25/shop.json/" download="shop.json">its shop data</a> into one directory. Run <code>python3 run-access.py --bindir /path/to/postgresql/bin</code>. It needs only Python’s standard library and PostgreSQL binaries. It creates and verifies a private temporary cluster, connects separately as each login and removes the cluster afterwards.</p>
<p>Verified on PostgreSQL 18.6 and Python 3.14.5. The private local socket uses trust authentication so the controlled test launcher can choose each identity. This verifies privileges under actual login sessions; it does not verify production password or certificate authentication, TLS, a proxy, credential storage or a pool’s context resets. The browser figure illustrates the two rules and does not execute SQL.</p>

